1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,582
During the pandemic, I got really into watching videos

2
00:00:02,582 --> 00:00:03,833
about Disney theme parks.

3
00:00:03,833 --> 00:00:06,461
How they're built, their history, upcoming developments,

4
00:00:06,461 --> 00:00:07,670
stuff like that.

5
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:08,838
As I worked from home,

6
00:00:08,838 --> 00:00:12,216
I would even put on these long, 4K video walkthroughs of the parks

7
00:00:12,216 --> 00:00:13,718
up on a second monitor

8
00:00:13,718 --> 00:00:18,181
and just let them play in the background like a window into another world.

9
00:00:18,181 --> 00:00:22,101
But it wasn't just the escapism that drew me to these places.

10
00:00:22,101 --> 00:00:26,814
As an artist, I saw the parks as unique studies of what happens when art,

11
00:00:26,814 --> 00:00:29,692
human experience and emotion were the highest priority

12
00:00:29,692 --> 00:00:31,944
when it came to designing a place.

13
00:00:31,944 --> 00:00:33,154
In the real world,

14
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:35,948
those are usually the first things cut from any budget.

15
00:00:35,948 --> 00:00:37,784
But at Disney theme parks,

16
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:40,161
they were essential.

17
00:00:40,161 --> 00:00:41,370
And because of this,

18
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:44,165
guests are able to immerse themselves in fantastical worlds

19
00:00:44,165 --> 00:00:45,875
and transcend their daily routines.

20
00:00:45,875 --> 00:00:48,294
They can embrace feelings of wonder and joy

21
00:00:48,294 --> 00:00:51,547
while creating lifelong memories with their family and friends.

22
00:00:51,547 --> 00:00:56,260
And to many, Disney theme parks truly are the happiest place on Earth.

23
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:59,305
See, nothing influences our lives more than place.

24
00:00:59,305 --> 00:01:01,557
Our surroundings affect our relationships,

25
00:01:01,557 --> 00:01:04,227
emotions, habits, even how long we'll live.

26
00:01:04,227 --> 00:01:09,148
So if we design places with care, purpose [and] the human experience in mind,

27
00:01:09,148 --> 00:01:10,650
we can nurture well-being,

28
00:01:10,650 --> 00:01:13,319
foster creativity and innovation,

29
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:16,572
[and] build stronger, more resilient communities.

30
00:01:16,572 --> 00:01:19,617
And while pockets of great places exist all over this country,

31
00:01:19,617 --> 00:01:22,495
most of America looks like this

32
00:01:22,495 --> 00:01:24,413
or this,

33
00:01:24,413 --> 00:01:26,165
or this.

34
00:01:26,165 --> 00:01:28,417
And these places aren't just aesthetically bad.

35
00:01:28,417 --> 00:01:32,964
They’re a drain on our physical, mental and economic health.

36
00:01:32,964 --> 00:01:35,133
But our places weren't always like this.

37
00:01:35,133 --> 00:01:37,385
American cities at the turn of the 20th century

38
00:01:37,385 --> 00:01:40,555
were designed for people to traverse, experience and understand.

39
00:01:40,555 --> 00:01:42,598
Work, food, school, whatever you might need

40
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:45,643
was only a short walk or trolley ride away.

41
00:01:45,643 --> 00:01:47,478
Developments and streets were smaller

42
00:01:47,478 --> 00:01:49,772
and folks weren't as spread out as they are now.

43
00:01:49,772 --> 00:01:52,567
And that meant you were more likely to know your neighbors

44
00:01:52,567 --> 00:01:56,112
and be more engaged with what was going on in your community.

45
00:01:56,112 --> 00:02:00,366
Downtown streets were shared by people, carriages, cyclists and vendors.

46
00:02:00,366 --> 00:02:02,201
And as they grew larger, more diverse,

47
00:02:02,201 --> 00:02:05,079
cities would develop their own unique identities and cultures

48
00:02:05,079 --> 00:02:08,124
that became sources of pride for anyone that called [them] home.

49
00:02:08,124 --> 00:02:09,709
And this wasn't like a new thing.

50
00:02:09,709 --> 00:02:11,711
For thousands of years across continents,

51
00:02:11,711 --> 00:02:13,588
this is how we built cities.

52
00:02:13,588 --> 00:02:15,381
But at the start of the 20th century,

53
00:02:15,381 --> 00:02:16,799
the way we built our places

54
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:20,678
and who or what they were built for would change dramatically

55
00:02:20,678 --> 00:02:23,806
due to one invention.

56
00:02:23,806 --> 00:02:25,600
From 1900 to 1915,

57
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,394
the number of automobiles in America went from 8,000

58
00:02:28,394 --> 00:02:30,062
to over two million.

59
00:02:30,062 --> 00:02:32,023
And while at first cars shared the road,

60
00:02:32,023 --> 00:02:35,359
it wasn't long before their dangerous high speeds and exhaust fumes

61
00:02:35,359 --> 00:02:40,156
drove vendors, cyclists and people back onto the sidewalks.

62
00:02:40,156 --> 00:02:43,868
The streets now belonged solely to the car.

63
00:02:43,868 --> 00:02:46,954
But this would only be the first change to the American landscape

64
00:02:46,954 --> 00:02:48,831
it would be responsible for.

65
00:02:48,831 --> 00:02:51,125
And just like Disney's "Cars,"

66
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:53,002
the second one was even worse.

67
00:02:53,002 --> 00:02:56,631
(Laughter)

68
00:02:56,631 --> 00:02:59,050
The economic boom in America after World War II

69
00:02:59,050 --> 00:03:02,053
led to a series of disastrous experiments in urban planning

70
00:03:02,053 --> 00:03:04,305
that continue to affect us today:

71
00:03:04,305 --> 00:03:05,556
the mass exodus of people

72
00:03:05,556 --> 00:03:08,059
from city centers to the suburbs on the edge of town,

73
00:03:08,059 --> 00:03:10,561
restrictive zoning laws that separated building uses,

74
00:03:10,561 --> 00:03:15,024
segregated people based on race or economic level

75
00:03:15,024 --> 00:03:17,360
and destructive government-funded projects

76
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:21,239
like highway expansions and urban renewal

77
00:03:21,239 --> 00:03:25,159
that demolished historic buildings, erased entire city blocks --

78
00:03:25,159 --> 00:03:30,331
usually just for more places to park their cars.

79
00:03:30,331 --> 00:03:32,333
So instead of designing places for people,

80
00:03:32,333 --> 00:03:34,627
we were now designing them for vehicles.

81
00:03:34,627 --> 00:03:36,712
And that trend continues to this day,

82
00:03:36,712 --> 00:03:39,548
on an even larger scale than before.

83
00:03:39,548 --> 00:03:43,094
And while over the past 30 years, we’ve seen the rebirth of many downtowns

84
00:03:43,094 --> 00:03:45,471
and historic neighborhoods, we need to do better.

85
00:03:45,471 --> 00:03:48,266
Small pockets of great places aren't enough.

86
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:51,352
And I think our communities deserve more.

87
00:03:51,352 --> 00:03:53,729
So as I sat in my sweatpants,

88
00:03:53,729 --> 00:03:58,150
watching my computer monitor longingly, wanting to be in these places,

89
00:03:58,150 --> 00:04:01,821
I started to wonder, why can't our cities be more like this?

90
00:04:01,821 --> 00:04:05,449
Why do these places only exist on vacation?

91
00:04:05,449 --> 00:04:09,578
What if we made our main streets more like Main Street, USA?

92
00:04:09,578 --> 00:04:11,497
So I want to share with you some ideas,

93
00:04:11,497 --> 00:04:13,708
techniques and lessons from Disney theme parks

94
00:04:13,708 --> 00:04:17,295
that communities of any size can use to build better places.

95
00:04:17,295 --> 00:04:20,423
And so I've broken these ideas up into five different categories:

96
00:04:20,423 --> 00:04:24,260
density, variety, walkability, theming and, of course, magic.

97
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:26,429
But let's start with density.

98
00:04:26,429 --> 00:04:29,890
Every Disney theme park is jam-packed with things to do and see.

99
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:33,311
Each land you visit is filled with a mix of rides, attractions, food --

100
00:04:33,311 --> 00:04:35,521
all within a few yards of each other.

101
00:04:35,521 --> 00:04:38,774
And this makes it easier to immerse yourself in the land's theming

102
00:04:38,774 --> 00:04:41,610
and helps create a more lively, engaging environment.

103
00:04:41,610 --> 00:04:45,448
Higher-density places offer greater opportunities for interaction

104
00:04:45,448 --> 00:04:47,783
and socialization.

105
00:04:47,783 --> 00:04:50,745
But density isn't just about building community

106
00:04:50,745 --> 00:04:53,331
or making friends [or] any of that.

107
00:04:53,331 --> 00:04:54,540
No, no, no.

108
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:56,917
Denser places also make us more money.

109
00:04:56,917 --> 00:05:00,755
Not only do existing businesses benefit from denser development

110
00:05:00,755 --> 00:05:02,214
due to higher foot traffic,

111
00:05:02,214 --> 00:05:04,842
but because of their more efficient land use,

112
00:05:04,842 --> 00:05:10,014
cities see significantly higher return on investment from denser development too.

113
00:05:10,014 --> 00:05:11,349
Take this local example.

114
00:05:11,349 --> 00:05:13,476
When we look at Zandbroz and Walmart,

115
00:05:13,476 --> 00:05:17,355
at first, Walmart seems like the more valuable development.

116
00:05:17,355 --> 00:05:22,151
But just like when we look at a car's miles-per-gallon to determine its value,

117
00:05:22,151 --> 00:05:25,905
we need to look at this parcel's value per acre.

118
00:05:25,905 --> 00:05:29,241
So when we divide these numbers by the amount of streets and utilities

119
00:05:29,241 --> 00:05:31,994
it takes to serve the entire property,

120
00:05:31,994 --> 00:05:37,792
we can see that Zandbroz is actually five times more valuable than Walmart.

121
00:05:37,792 --> 00:05:41,504
And this is what it looks like if you map the entire value-per-acre for a city.

122
00:05:41,504 --> 00:05:42,880
And everything in green,

123
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:45,174
the city is either making limited money on

124
00:05:45,174 --> 00:05:47,760
or even losing money on.

125
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:50,304
And as you can see, downtown and denser developments

126
00:05:50,304 --> 00:05:54,892
are actually subsidizing the rest of the city.

127
00:05:54,892 --> 00:05:56,894
So now that we know why density matters,

128
00:05:56,894 --> 00:05:59,605
where and how do we start applying it in our communities?

129
00:05:59,605 --> 00:06:02,983
It's impossible to spread these ideas out evenly across an entire city.

130
00:06:02,983 --> 00:06:06,278
So it's important to focus on places where things are already working,

131
00:06:06,278 --> 00:06:08,030
where people are already gathering.

132
00:06:08,030 --> 00:06:10,408
And usually that place is somewhere downtown.

133
00:06:10,408 --> 00:06:12,743
So first we need to start by filling in the gaps.

134
00:06:12,743 --> 00:06:15,246
Vacant lots, empty storefronts, parking lots.

135
00:06:15,246 --> 00:06:17,164
They're sprinkled throughout downtowns,

136
00:06:17,164 --> 00:06:19,125
and while they may seem innocuous enough,

137
00:06:19,125 --> 00:06:23,212
their lack of life can repel people from exploring further on foot.

138
00:06:23,212 --> 00:06:27,049
And filling in these gaps with mixed-use buildings is the ideal first choice.

139
00:06:27,049 --> 00:06:29,635
But new construction and renovations can be expensive,

140
00:06:29,635 --> 00:06:33,055
and I know that's not always an option for some communities.

141
00:06:33,055 --> 00:06:34,348
So if money is a concern,

142
00:06:34,348 --> 00:06:37,309
there are plenty of other options that require smaller budgets

143
00:06:37,309 --> 00:06:38,644
that can make a big impact.

144
00:06:38,644 --> 00:06:41,772
Parklets, community gardens, outdoor stages, food trucks,

145
00:06:41,772 --> 00:06:43,357
along with regular programming,

146
00:06:43,357 --> 00:06:48,654
are great ways to take empty spaces and turn them into ones filled with life.

147
00:06:48,654 --> 00:06:51,907
They say variety is the spice of life, and if that's true,

148
00:06:51,907 --> 00:06:54,994
then places like this would be flour.

149
00:06:54,994 --> 00:06:56,162
(Laughter)

150
00:06:56,162 --> 00:07:01,041
Variety adds beauty, vibrancy and interest to the built environment.

151
00:07:01,041 --> 00:07:03,794
Main Street, USA is the perfect example of variety.

152
00:07:03,794 --> 00:07:06,046
Dozens of uniquely styled, colorful buildings

153
00:07:06,046 --> 00:07:09,091
filled with different kinds of businesses line the busy streets.

154
00:07:09,091 --> 00:07:11,302
And when used well, variety builds excitement

155
00:07:11,302 --> 00:07:14,180
and makes people want to explore further.

156
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:16,724
And when used really well, variety builds excitement,

157
00:07:16,724 --> 00:07:21,562
makes people want to turn that next corner.

158
00:07:21,562 --> 00:07:24,732
So here's how we can add a little bit more variety to our places.

159
00:07:24,732 --> 00:07:26,942
First, mixed-use space is one of the best ways

160
00:07:26,942 --> 00:07:30,154
to activate your neighborhood 24/7

161
00:07:30,154 --> 00:07:33,532
and keep [it] activated throughout the day.

162
00:07:33,532 --> 00:07:37,203
Next, this is what downtown parcels used to look like.

163
00:07:37,203 --> 00:07:39,455
And this is what they look like now.

164
00:07:39,455 --> 00:07:42,500
Instead of multiple architects designing these places over time,

165
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:46,212
we now have a single developer building entire blocks at a time.

166
00:07:46,212 --> 00:07:48,881
Not only does this price out smaller developers,

167
00:07:48,881 --> 00:07:52,009
it also tends to make downtown feel more like an office park

168
00:07:52,009 --> 00:07:55,721
rather than the charming main streets we all know and love.

169
00:07:55,721 --> 00:07:57,973
Finally, using a variety of colors and styles

170
00:07:57,973 --> 00:08:00,643
is essential to creating engaging places.

171
00:08:00,643 --> 00:08:03,229
When streets are lined with identical forms like this,

172
00:08:03,229 --> 00:08:06,232
people tend to either stay away or stay inside.

173
00:08:06,232 --> 00:08:08,359
By differentiating the look of your facades,

174
00:08:08,359 --> 00:08:12,071
you can create places that are much more interesting.

175
00:08:12,071 --> 00:08:14,990
In fact, Main Street, USA is actually closer to a strip mall

176
00:08:14,990 --> 00:08:17,159
than it is to a traditional downtown street,

177
00:08:17,159 --> 00:08:20,663
but you'd never know it from the outside.

178
00:08:20,663 --> 00:08:23,499
So Disney theme parks are full of some of the most walkable

179
00:08:23,499 --> 00:08:25,459
pedestrian places on Earth.

180
00:08:25,459 --> 00:08:28,462
You're able to enjoy all these different places and attractions

181
00:08:28,462 --> 00:08:30,839
without having to worry about loading up your car,

182
00:08:30,839 --> 00:08:33,175
driving through traffic, finding a place to park,

183
00:08:33,175 --> 00:08:36,595
and then doing it all over again when you want to go somewhere else.

184
00:08:36,595 --> 00:08:39,223
And beyond looking out for the occasional parade float,

185
00:08:39,223 --> 00:08:42,851
children are able to explore the environment freely and safely.

186
00:08:42,851 --> 00:08:44,812
Which leads me [to] why it’s so important

187
00:08:44,812 --> 00:08:48,232
that we focus on making our streets more walkable.

188
00:08:48,232 --> 00:08:50,442
This graph shows the likelihood of survival

189
00:08:50,442 --> 00:08:52,736
when a pedestrian is hit by a car.

190
00:08:52,736 --> 00:08:57,866
As the vehicle speed increases, the odds of dying grow exponentially.

191
00:08:57,866 --> 00:09:01,495
If someone you love is hit by a car going 40 miles an hour,

192
00:09:01,495 --> 00:09:05,291
they only have a 15 percent chance of surviving.

193
00:09:05,291 --> 00:09:09,169
By changing the geometry of the streets through road diets,

194
00:09:09,169 --> 00:09:12,965
we can create places that naturally encourage safer driving.

195
00:09:12,965 --> 00:09:15,593
Here's where we can start.

196
00:09:15,593 --> 00:09:18,804
The wider the traffic lane, the faster the car.

197
00:09:18,804 --> 00:09:22,683
Traffic lanes inside city limits shouldn’t be wider than 10 feet.

198
00:09:22,683 --> 00:09:25,144
They're usually 11 in Sioux Falls.

199
00:09:25,144 --> 00:09:28,397
Anything more than that encourages speeding.

200
00:09:28,397 --> 00:09:30,441
Converting four-lane roads like this one

201
00:09:30,441 --> 00:09:32,860
into three-lane roads like this

202
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:35,696
has been shown to actually improve traffic flow

203
00:09:35,696 --> 00:09:37,615
and reduce the potential for accidents.

204
00:09:37,615 --> 00:09:38,991
In fact, one study showed

205
00:09:38,991 --> 00:09:41,327
that when they did this four-to-three conversion,

206
00:09:41,327 --> 00:09:45,247
accidents dropped by 47 percent.

207
00:09:45,247 --> 00:09:48,334
Painting your parking lane lines can visually shrink the streets

208
00:09:48,334 --> 00:09:50,419
and help slow speeding traffic.

209
00:09:50,419 --> 00:09:52,963
A few years ago, I worked with the city of Sioux Falls

210
00:09:52,963 --> 00:09:54,923
to add parking lane lines on 18th Street

211
00:09:54,923 --> 00:09:57,593
between Minnesota Avenue and Avera Hospital.

212
00:09:57,593 --> 00:10:01,764
And the average speed dropped from 33 miles per hour to 28.

213
00:10:01,764 --> 00:10:03,849
And that might not seem like a lot,

214
00:10:03,849 --> 00:10:05,643
but when you think about that chart,

215
00:10:05,643 --> 00:10:09,104
remember that every little bit could mean the difference

216
00:10:09,104 --> 00:10:12,399
between life and death.

217
00:10:12,399 --> 00:10:15,361
Adding bump-outs and painting crosswalks at major intersections

218
00:10:15,361 --> 00:10:17,529
can shorten crossing distance between corners

219
00:10:17,529 --> 00:10:20,199
and give pedestrians greater visibility.

220
00:10:20,199 --> 00:10:23,327
I’d also like to point out, smaller roads mean less snow to plow

221
00:10:23,327 --> 00:10:26,538
and fewer potholes to fill each spring.

222
00:10:26,538 --> 00:10:28,666
Something to think about.

223
00:10:28,666 --> 00:10:30,209
Now that the roads are smaller,

224
00:10:30,209 --> 00:10:32,920
we have room to add more useful things like street trees,

225
00:10:32,920 --> 00:10:35,130
plants, bike lanes and even angled parking.

226
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:37,007
And now we're getting to the fun stuff.

227
00:10:37,007 --> 00:10:39,218
Theming is the art of creating a cohesive

228
00:10:39,218 --> 00:10:42,763
and immersive environment that enhances the guest experience.

229
00:10:42,763 --> 00:10:46,350
Theming is crucial to telling a land’s story, setting the mood

230
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:48,977
and creating a unique sense of place.

231
00:10:48,977 --> 00:10:51,939
But how do we theme a city?

232
00:10:51,939 --> 00:10:54,942
Every land, ride and restaurant in a Disney theme park

233
00:10:54,942 --> 00:10:57,653
has a detailed backstory, imagined history,

234
00:10:57,653 --> 00:10:59,655
and it's communicated through its layout,

235
00:10:59,655 --> 00:11:02,199
its architecture, its food, and a dozen other details

236
00:11:02,199 --> 00:11:03,826
you'll probably never even notice.

237
00:11:03,826 --> 00:11:07,663
But they all come together to form places with distinct identities.

238
00:11:07,663 --> 00:11:10,040
Preserving the history, buildings and culture

239
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:12,334
that made your community the place it is today

240
00:11:12,334 --> 00:11:16,755
is an essential part of creating unique spaces.

241
00:11:16,755 --> 00:11:19,925
One way we can do that is by preserving our existing places,

242
00:11:19,925 --> 00:11:22,177
the houses, buildings, bridges, barns,

243
00:11:22,177 --> 00:11:26,140
even the trees around us create the settings for our life stories.

244
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:30,018
And as the years go on, we form emotional connections with these things.

245
00:11:30,018 --> 00:11:32,187
So when they're torn down,

246
00:11:32,187 --> 00:11:34,940
we're also severing people's connection to their community

247
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:36,900
and to their own past.

248
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:39,778
So prioritize filling in your existing gaps

249
00:11:39,778 --> 00:11:42,406
before you start making new ones.

250
00:11:42,406 --> 00:11:45,409
Disney parks are full of iconic buildings [and] structures,

251
00:11:45,409 --> 00:11:47,244
and they act as symbols for the parks.

252
00:11:47,244 --> 00:11:49,288
Due to their size and eye-catching designs,

253
00:11:49,288 --> 00:11:51,582
they act as beacons, urging guests forward.

254
00:11:51,582 --> 00:11:54,543
Inspired by the way his dog would follow him from room to room

255
00:11:54,543 --> 00:11:56,420
whenever he was snacking on a hot dog,

256
00:11:56,420 --> 00:12:00,340
Walt Disney would refer to these buildings as “weenies.”

257
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:03,010
And Sioux Falls has no shortage of iconic weenies,

258
00:12:03,010 --> 00:12:04,762
like the old Courthouse Museum,

259
00:12:04,762 --> 00:12:06,972
the Cathedral, The Arc of Dreams.

260
00:12:06,972 --> 00:12:08,182
We're also home

261
00:12:08,182 --> 00:12:11,310
to an internationally famous weenie, the Statue of David.

262
00:12:11,310 --> 00:12:15,773
(Laughter)

263
00:12:15,773 --> 00:12:19,276
In rural towns, water towers, churches, silos,

264
00:12:19,276 --> 00:12:23,280
they can all act in that same purpose, like in Faulkton, South Dakota,

265
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:26,116
where this amazing mural can be seen from miles away.

266
00:12:26,116 --> 00:12:29,369
And it's the envy of any town with a grain elevator.

267
00:12:29,369 --> 00:12:32,873
Speaking of murals, they’re a great way to communicate a place’s identity.

268
00:12:32,873 --> 00:12:35,417
I paint a lot of murals in cities across South Dakota,

269
00:12:35,417 --> 00:12:37,920
and some of my favorite to do are these postcard ones

270
00:12:37,920 --> 00:12:39,713
where I get to work with communities

271
00:12:39,713 --> 00:12:42,841
to showcase the history, places and people that make them unique,

272
00:12:42,841 --> 00:12:46,178
like the Rodeo in Clear Lake or Centerville’s zebra doughnuts.

273
00:12:46,178 --> 00:12:50,390
City flags can represent a place’s history, culture and aspirations,

274
00:12:50,390 --> 00:12:54,144
and a well-designed flag can evoke a sense of pride and belonging

275
00:12:54,144 --> 00:12:56,230
in the people that live there.

276
00:12:56,230 --> 00:12:59,066
And there are few cities that have embraced their flag more

277
00:12:59,066 --> 00:13:01,944
than the city of Sioux Falls.

278
00:13:01,944 --> 00:13:04,446
Cities are filled with ugly stuff.

279
00:13:04,446 --> 00:13:06,156
When things like lights, speakers,

280
00:13:06,156 --> 00:13:09,660
or outside buildings break the reality of [a] land’s theming,

281
00:13:09,660 --> 00:13:12,246
Disney uses a color called "Go Away Green"

282
00:13:12,246 --> 00:13:15,874
to make that item fade into the background and disappear.

283
00:13:15,874 --> 00:13:20,045
And you can use colors to hide things or make them part of the theme.

284
00:13:20,045 --> 00:13:23,423
You can also draw more attention to them by making them into artwork.

285
00:13:23,423 --> 00:13:26,426
These are some light pole wraps I designed for my neighborhood

286
00:13:26,426 --> 00:13:29,263
that are meant to look like Sioux Quartzite stone from afar,

287
00:13:29,263 --> 00:13:32,099
but up close are filled with pictures of children's artwork,

288
00:13:32,099 --> 00:13:33,350
historic photos of homes,

289
00:13:33,350 --> 00:13:37,145
and pictures of the people who live in the neighborhood.

290
00:13:37,145 --> 00:13:39,857
The last category I want to talk about tonight is magic,

291
00:13:39,857 --> 00:13:42,067
and this one can be a little harder to define.

292
00:13:42,067 --> 00:13:47,197
Disney Magic is created by the combination of unique sights, sounds and smells,

293
00:13:47,197 --> 00:13:48,657
by attention to detail,

294
00:13:48,657 --> 00:13:52,035
and the energetic atmosphere found in every corner of the parks.

295
00:13:52,035 --> 00:13:55,038
Magic is the thing that keeps people coming back for more,

296
00:13:55,038 --> 00:13:57,291
and makes them fall in love with a place.

297
00:13:57,291 --> 00:14:02,170
And we can make magic happen in our places in big and small ways.

298
00:14:02,170 --> 00:14:03,839
Public art is great,

299
00:14:03,839 --> 00:14:06,383
but public art isn't just murals and statues.

300
00:14:06,383 --> 00:14:08,635
So why have a normal garbage can on the beach

301
00:14:08,635 --> 00:14:11,638
when you could feed it to a fish named Goby?

302
00:14:11,638 --> 00:14:15,225
Or if you’re pouring a new sidewalk, why not add some local poetry?

303
00:14:15,225 --> 00:14:18,061
Integrating art in a unique or unexpected way

304
00:14:18,061 --> 00:14:22,441
can turn ordinary cities into extraordinary ones.

305
00:14:22,441 --> 00:14:25,652
"Streetmosphere" is the term Disney uses for the various performers

306
00:14:25,652 --> 00:14:27,946
and sights you might see at the parks.

307
00:14:27,946 --> 00:14:30,574
And great places have energetic streets.

308
00:14:30,574 --> 00:14:34,536
And you can make your streets more active by adding outdoor seating or patios,

309
00:14:34,536 --> 00:14:36,038
hiring street musicians,

310
00:14:36,038 --> 00:14:39,249
or you can close down streets for special events like block parties

311
00:14:39,249 --> 00:14:42,461
or parades or farmers markets.

312
00:14:42,461 --> 00:14:44,504
Disney is not the only home --

313
00:14:44,504 --> 00:14:47,382
the only place that's home to iconic costumed characters

314
00:14:47,382 --> 00:14:51,136
that generations of people know and love.

315
00:14:51,136 --> 00:14:54,348
Mascots from your city sports teams, and high schools,

316
00:14:54,348 --> 00:14:56,308
as well as beloved TV personalities,

317
00:14:56,308 --> 00:14:59,311
can all play a part in developing a city's identity.

318
00:14:59,311 --> 00:15:04,191
And just like flags, they can become symbols of pride for communities too.

319
00:15:04,191 --> 00:15:07,527
Disney goes all out during holidays and celebrations with decorations,

320
00:15:07,527 --> 00:15:10,030
seasonal characters, limited-time-only food.

321
00:15:10,030 --> 00:15:13,158
In your community, little efforts like hanging some lights

322
00:15:13,158 --> 00:15:15,535
or tailgating before the big homecoming game

323
00:15:15,535 --> 00:15:19,289
are ways to communicate to visitors and to the people that live there

324
00:15:19,289 --> 00:15:23,126
that their town is alive and loved.

325
00:15:23,126 --> 00:15:26,463
And this is another place where I get to say Sioux Falls gets it right.

326
00:15:26,463 --> 00:15:28,966
When it comes to the holidays, we don't mess around.

327
00:15:28,966 --> 00:15:30,592
(Laughter)

328
00:15:30,592 --> 00:15:32,719
Years before Disneyland opened,

329
00:15:32,719 --> 00:15:35,472
every Saturday, Walt would take his daughters around town

330
00:15:35,472 --> 00:15:37,307
for some quality time together.

331
00:15:37,307 --> 00:15:41,561
They’d visit amusement parks, playgrounds, carousels, which the girls loved.

332
00:15:41,561 --> 00:15:45,524
But Walt would inevitably find himself alone on a park bench

333
00:15:45,524 --> 00:15:48,944
with all the other adults watching their kids play.

334
00:15:48,944 --> 00:15:50,195
And he thought to himself,

335
00:15:50,195 --> 00:15:51,822
there should be something built

336
00:15:51,822 --> 00:15:55,242
where parents and children could have fun together.

337
00:15:55,242 --> 00:15:58,078
And eventually, that idea evolved into Disneyland,

338
00:15:58,078 --> 00:16:03,250
a place where people of all ages could come together, create lasting memories,

339
00:16:03,250 --> 00:16:05,877
where imagination could become reality,

340
00:16:05,877 --> 00:16:09,006
where dreams could come true.

341
00:16:09,006 --> 00:16:12,884
But I want to challenge you to dream bigger than Walt Disney.

342
00:16:12,884 --> 00:16:16,513
These ideas don't have to stay on vacation.

343
00:16:16,513 --> 00:16:20,726
If we design places with care, purpose and for people,

344
00:16:20,726 --> 00:16:23,937
any community can become the happiest place on Earth.

345
00:16:23,937 --> 00:16:25,147
Thank you, Sioux Falls.

346
00:16:25,147 --> 00:16:29,147
(Applause)
